#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162954} :activity/id #uuid "33fa95a4-1ec1-5d26-8a37-443af950cbe7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048724 :topic/id #uuid "b89537b6-44d1-540c-9c66-f20f2c0c2412" :topic/math-academy-id 3056} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163264 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable whose probability density function is known. We know that if $Y = u(X)$, then we can apply the distribution function method to compute the PDF of $Y$.\n\nIt's possible to generalize the distribution function method when $u(X)$ is strictly monotonic. This generalization is known as the **change-of-variables method**, and is stated below:\n\n> *If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X)$, where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by*\n> ${f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}(u^{-1})^{′}(y) &,\\end{vmatrix}yis betweenu(a)andu(b); 0, \\mid otherwise$.\n\nProof of this result will be provided at the end of the lesson.\n\nThe advantage of the change-of-variables method is that it's often easier and faster to implement than the distribution function method. The disadvantage is that it only works when $u$ is monotonic, whereas the distribution function method can be extended to deal with non-monotonic functions.\n\nFor example, suppose that $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y = 3X$. Then $u(x) = 3x$, which is strictly monotonically increasing. Therefore, the change-of-variables method can be used to compute the PDF of $Y$.\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(y) &= 3y \\\\\ny &= 3u^{-1}(y) \\\\\nu^{-1}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\nu^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{1}{3}y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad (u^{-1})'(y) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero $f_Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot \\mid (u^{-1})^{′}(y) \\mid \\\\\n&= f_{X}(\\frac{1}{3}y) \\cdot \\mid \\frac{1}{3} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}y) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 2$, the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2$, and we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\qquad u(2) = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{18}y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < 6 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86b37435-f432-5855-9152-51435d0e102c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5356 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4d93e146-01aa-543e-9cbb-e868641f90cd" :step/math-academy-id 33486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162052 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161852 :question/id #uuid "14830c55-32bd-5eb9-adb6-b91675061a21" :question/math-academy-id "e-9100"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162278}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable"} :step/id #uuid "b79655f1-bb54-5c12-b761-06cd72a1c5ae" :step/math-academy-id 33924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162967}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161853 :question/id #uuid "2fd76ea7-ab08-5afa-9762-158a85999321" :question/math-academy-id "e-9101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050322}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162399}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "d5dfe3a6-d6eb-5bee-97c5-ade0ebcb344a" :step/math-academy-id 33925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161854 :question/id #uuid "fd8ceb2f-55e5-588f-ae79-015f58007fba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9102"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049860}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162406}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "18b53d37-ee53-59da-9080-3399c63c1c76" :step/math-academy-id 33926 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163272 :tutorial/content "Let's restate the change-of-variables method:\n\n> *If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X)$, where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by*\n> ${f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}(u^{-1})^{′}(y) &,\\end{vmatrix}yis betweenu(a)andu(b); 0, \\mid otherwise$.\n\nNow, let's prove this result.\n\nThe first part is simple. Because $X$ is between $a$ and $b$, and $u$ is monotonic, $Y=u(X)$ must be between $u(a)$ and $u(b)$. So\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $y$ is not between $u(a)$ and $u(b)$.\n\nTo carry out the rest of the proof, we will need to apply the distribution function method in two cases:\n\n- **Case 1**: $u$ is monotonically increasing\n- **Case 2**: $u$ is monotonically decreasing\n\nWe'll consider the case where $u$ is monotonically increasing only. The proof when $u$ is monotonically decreasing is similar.\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative distribution of $Y$. Assuming $y$ is between $u(a)$ and $u(b)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(u(X) \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le u^{-1}(y)) \\\\\n&= \\int_{a}^{u^{-1}(y)}f_{X}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\left.F_{X}(t)\\right|_{a}^{u^{-1}(y)} \\\\\n&= F_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) - F_{X}(a)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we differentiate to get the probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dy}[F_{Y}(y)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}[F_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) - F_{X}(a)] \\\\\n&= f_{X}(u^{-1}(y))(u^{-1})^{′}(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $u$ is monotonically increasing, we have that $u^{-1}$ is monotonically increasing as well. To see this, it's helpful to remember that we reflect a function over the line $y=x$ to get its inverse.\n\nSince the slope of a monotonically increasing function is always zero or positive, we have\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand consequently\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) = \\left| (u^{-1})'(y) \\right|\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= f_{X}(u^{-1}(y)) \\mid (u^{-1})^{′}(y) \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "20e03906-77e2-525c-8b4e-ca6bd91782de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5449 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Change-of-Variables Formula"} :step/id #uuid "95cd8c7f-a4dd-5ba6-ad88-eab8ac6726e7" :step/math-academy-id 33927}] :activity/title "The Change-of-Variables Method for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162192}]}
